alpha" lesson were embedded in the era, one would expect a burst of
3-fold atomic-style designs in 2000 — instead, 2000's headliners are 4-fold/grid, 44-circle and 22-fold. The
3-fold designs in our dataset cluster in 1991–1997 (tetrahedron, triple Julia, fractal triangles) — symmetry
fashion, not a carbon curriculum.</p>
<div class="panel"><p class="chart-title">Source forensics</p>
<p class="note">The video itself leaves no public footprint: the phrases "three-alpha lock" and "2.17 mm" return zero
indexed results; no forum thread (Reddit, Metabunk, ATS) discusses it. Closest relatives in the decode genre:
the IASOR "TIFACS" proposal (Chilbolton as carbon/silicon biochemistry), Matti Pitkänen's TGD physics, and
Horace Drew's binary decodings. The debunking apparatus that applies is the standard one: Nickell 1996 and
Grassi et al. 2005 on node-measurement methodology, plus the documented human origins of the era's landmarks.</p></div>

<div class="panel hyp"><p class="chart-title">Verdict</p>
<p class="note">The video wraps one of physics' genuine wonders — the Hoyle state, on which the carbon abundance of
the universe hangs — in fabricated terminology, a physically meaningless gravity link, a non-existent measurement,
and a season count that no archive supports. The real science is more astonishing than the embellishment: a
resonance 285 keV wide deciding whether stars make carbon. It deserves better than a "three-alpha lock."</p>
<p class="verdict rejected">Overall verdict: DISPROVEN (counts, atomic theme, 2.17 mm, gravity link) · with UNFALSIFIABLE elements (implant) riding on GARBLED real science (triple-alpha, Hoyle state, cluster geometry)</p></div>
